  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 252 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nVery little has changed regarding the long term forecast, as \nnorthwesterly to quasi-zonal flow aloft will persist for several \ndays, before a broad upper ridge slowly moves east from the western \nCONUS late in the week. Our forecast confidence remains high that \ngeopotential height rises from the slowly encroaching ridge will \nresult in a gradual warming trend from Tuesday onward, and \ntemperatures area-wide will climb back above average. Exactly how \nfar above average is less certain, and will depend on a number of \nsubtle factors. \n\nNamely, the strength and speed of the ridge as it moves east late in \nthe week seems to be the biggest source of uncertainty, combined \nwith perhaps some bias from model forecast suites. Regarding the \nformer, in spite of the relatively uneventful pattern that tends to \nresult in a high degree of medium/long range predictability, \nensemble temperature forecast spreads remain relatively high from \nmid to late week. This appears to be linked to subtle differences in \nthe strength of the ridge, as a stronger ridge would result in more \nsubstantial warming. While ensemble means remain generally in the \nlow 80s for the most part, this comes with roughly 7-10 degrees of \nspread between the 25th-75th percentile, with some notably cool \noutliers still present as well. So, while we still expect (80+%) to \nsee multiple days of above average temperatures Tuesday onward, this \ncould mean anything from just a couple of degrees warmer than we are \nnow, to the upper 80s and maybe even touching 90 degrees again in a \nfew places. Not only this, but there remains some slight differences \nin temperature forecasts due to model bias (namely the relatively \nwarm GEFS and possibly some influence from bias correction). In any \ncase though, these differences are relatively small, and generally \nnot impactful in a practical sense. \n\nOtherwise, low humidity will persist for the foreseeable \nfuture, and there remains very little opportunity for \nprecipitation for the next week. This may change next week as it\nappears that the ridge will give way to southwest flow again as\nit moves east, but confidence in the timing of this transition \nremains low.",
